==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|þakka}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*þankōną}}. Cognates include {{cog|fo|takka}}, [[w:Danish|Danish]] and [[w:Norwegian|Norwegian]] ''[[takke]]'', {{cog|sv|tacka}}, {{cog|en|thank}}, {{cog|nl|danken}} and {{cog|de|danken}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈθahka/}}
* {{audio|is|Is-þakka.ogg}}
*: {{rhymes|is|ahka|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{is-verb-weak|þakkaði|þakkað}}

# to [[thank]]

====Conjugation====
{{is-conj-w4|þ|a|kk}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|is|flytja}} þökk (“bring thanks”)
* {{l|is|færa}} þökk (“bring thanks”)
* {{l|is|gjalda}} þökk (“pay thanks”)
* {{l|is|tjá}} þökk (“express thanks”)

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|þakka þér}} {{qualifier|singular}} (literally “(I) thank you”)
** {{l|is|þakka þér fyrir}} {{qualifier|singular}} (literally “thank you ''for'' ...”)
** {{l|is|þakka ykkur fyrir}} {{qualifier|plural}} (literally “thank you ''for'' ...”)
* {{l|is|þakka yður}} {{qualifier|singular, formal; rare}} (literally “(I) thank you ...”)
** {{l|is|þakka yður fyrir}} {{qualifier|singular and plural, formal; rare}} (literally “thank you ''for'' ...”)

====Related terms====
* {{l|is|þökk||''a thank'', thanks}}

==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*þankōną}}, akin to Old English {{m|ang|þancian}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|thankon}}, Old High German {{m|goh|dankōn}}.

===Verb===
{{non-verb|þakkaði|þǫkkuðu|þakkaðr}}

# to [[thank]], to show gratitude
##: {{ux|non|Konan '''þakkaði''' honum vel gjǫfina.|She '''thanked''' him well for the gift.}}

====Usage notes====
* Unlike in English, where the direct object of the verb "to thank" is the person being thanked and the indirect object is the thing being thanked for, in Old Norse, the thing being thanked for is the accusative object and the person being thanked is marked with the dative, as seen in the example above from page 70 of ''A New Introduction to Old Norse'' by Michael Barnes.

====Conjugation====
{{non-conj-weak2|þakk|þǫkk}}

====Related terms====
